/* Original Glasshouse visual reading guides. These annotate the existing model;
   they do not prescribe new motion, change its physics, or replace an exercise solution. */
(function(){'use strict';
const G={};
const add=(section,intuition,watch,connection,limit,question)=>{G[section]={intuition,watch,connection,limit,question};};
add('21.1','Two charges exert one interaction on each other, with equal force magnitudes even when the charges differ.','Switch either charge sign, then increase separation while comparing both force arrows.','The signed product q₁q₂ determines attraction or repulsion; 1/r² determines how force weakens with distance.','The charges are held at chosen positions. The display does not integrate their acceleration or predict an orbit.','If one charge is twice the other, which experiences the larger interaction force?');
add('21.2','Charge transfer arrives in whole elementary-charge units, with an electron carrying a negative unit.','Add one electron at a time and compare the final sign markers with the integer inventory.','Δq = −Ne converts the counted added electrons into signed charge; e = 1.602176634×10⁻¹⁹ C.','The elementary charge is not a fractional-electron control. A positive object has an electron deficit, not extra positive electrons.','Can adding electrons make an initially positive object neutral before making it negative?');
add('21.3','Moving electrons redistributes charge between objects without changing the closed system’s total.','Transfer electrons from A to B, then reverse transfer; follow both object inventories and their unchanged sum.','Each added electron changes B by −e and A by +e, so the two charge changes cancel exactly.','The chosen system includes both objects and transfer path. No charge enters from the environment.','Why does the object losing negative electrons become more positive?');
add('22.1','The field belongs to the source; force also depends on what you put into it.','Keep E fixed and change the probe from positive to negative: the coral force reverses while the aqua field does not.','F = qE multiplies the same source field by the signed probe charge.','A zero-charge probe feels zero force; it does not prove the external field is zero. The source is undisturbed here.','If the probe charge doubles, which arrow should double and which should stay unchanged?');
add('22.2','A point source spreads its influence over spherical area, so distance matters twice.','Double the probe radius and compare the signed radial-field reading. Rotate at fixed radius to isolate direction from magnitude.','E_r = kq/r² makes twice the distance give one quarter the magnitude; the radial unit vector supplies direction.','The moving probe is held by a measuring arm. Its circular scan is not a predicted charged-particle orbit.','Why does rotating the arm change the arrow direction without changing the field magnitude?');
add('22.3','Two individually strong fields can nearly cancel, leaving a much smaller dipole field.','Follow the two source-to-probe distances; the nearer positive source wins on the chosen outer axis.','Subtract the two inverse-square terms first. Only far away does their difference reduce to 2kqd/z³.','The far-field expression needs z much larger than d; it is not the exact answer near either charge.','As z/d grows, why should exact and approximate readings approach one another?');
add('22.4','Symmetry removes sideways field before any integration is needed.','The highlighted opposite ring elements have equal distances to the probe. Their sideways contributions cancel pairwise.','Each axial contribution contains z/√(R²+z²); summing yields E_z = kQz/(R²+z²)^(3/2).','At the center z = 0, the field is zero even though every source element remains charged.','Why can the center field vanish while the field a little along the axis is nonzero?');
add('22.5','A disk can be assembled mathematically from thin charged rings.','Watch the highlighted ring radius while changing the disk radius at fixed surface density. More disk also means more total charge.','The ring charge is dq = σ2πr dr. Its axial contributions integrate to the displayed finite-disk field.','The infinite-sheet limit is approached when disk radius is large compared with probe height; a finite disk is not exactly an infinite sheet.','At fixed σ and z, should adding outer rings increase or decrease the field magnitude?');
add('22.6','The electric field sets a force, mass turns it into acceleration, and time accumulates the displacement.','Start from rest; compare the signed force arrow with the direction of the bead’s travel as you scrub time.','F_x = qE_x and a_x = F_x/m lead to v_x = a_xt and Δx = a_xt²/2.','This is physical low-speed motion from rest. Gravity, drag and relativistic effects are excluded.','At the same elapsed time, what happens to travel distance if mass doubles?');
add('22.7','Two opposite forces can cancel in translation while still turning an object.','Turn the dipole through alignment, perpendicular orientation and anti-alignment; compare torque with orientation energy.','The separation creates p = qd; the lever arm gives τ_z = −pE sinθ while U = −pE cosθ.','The angle is imposed, not obtained from an inertia-and-damping simulation. Zero torque includes unstable anti-alignment.','Why is zero torque at 180° different from zero torque at 0°?');
add('23.1','Flux measures how much field pierces an oriented surface, not how much field lies beside it.','Rotate the coral normal through 90° while the aqua field stays horizontal. The sign changes only after crossing 90°.','EA cosθ is the surface area times the field component along its normal.','At 90° the flux is zero although the field and surface area are both nonzero; no charge flow is represented.','Would doubling area restore nonzero flux when the field lies exactly parallel to the screen?');
add('23.2','A closed surface counts net source charge inside, even when outside sources bend its local field.','Move the second charge outside, then change its value: local arrows change while its net-flux contribution stays zero.','The complete closed-surface integral equals q_enclosed/ε₀. Entering and leaving flux from an external source cancel.','The dashed circle is a spherical cross-section. Zero net flux does not imply zero field at every point.','Can two different-looking boundary fields have exactly the same total flux?');
add('23.3','The conductor rearranges surface charge until its own material has zero electrostatic field.','Move the probe through cavity, shaded metal and outside; compare induced inner charge with outer charge.','A Gaussian surface in metal requires q_cavity + Q_inner = 0. Charge conservation then gives Q_outer = Q_net + q_cavity.','The centered charge and spherical boundaries make this a symmetric special case. Interface values use the stated outward-side convention.','If the conductor is neutral, why can both its inner and outer surfaces still carry charge?');
add('23.4','The Gaussian sleeve is a counting boundary, not an extra physical cylinder.','Change only sleeve length: enclosed charge and curved area both grow, leaving the local radial field unchanged.','Gauss’s law divides λL times the enclosed cross-section fraction by 2πrL; L cancels.','Inside the uniform rod, enclosed fraction is r²/R². End effects are neglected in this long-cylinder model.','Why does field grow with r inside the rod but fall with r outside?');
add('23.5','A single ideal sheet divides its field between two sides; opposite sheets reinforce only between them.','Add the second sheet, then move the probe beyond it. The outside arrows cancel rather than fading gradually with distance.','One-sheet flux is 2EA = σA/ε₀. Superposition doubles E between opposite sheets and cancels it outside.','Infinite sheets have no edge effects. Pillbox area changes flux and enclosed charge together, so it does not change E.','Why does a larger pillbox enclose more charge without measuring a larger field?');
add('23.6','The Gaussian surface responds only to the portion of the charge distribution it encloses.','Switch ball to shell with Q and R fixed, then move the dashed radius inside and outside.','For a ball Q_enclosed ∝ r³ and Gaussian area ∝ r², giving E ∝ r inside. A shell encloses no charge in its empty interior.','Both distributions give the same external kQ/r² field; the thin-shell surface uses its outward limiting field.','Can an external field measurement distinguish this shell from this uniform ball?');
add('24.1','Voltage is energy per charge; the probe’s sign decides whether the same voltage drop raises or lowers its energy.','Carry the probe toward the dashed destination, then reverse q while keeping the plate voltages fixed.','ΔU = qΔV and W_field = −ΔU connect the voltage change to the two opposite work readings.','The external positioner keeps kinetic energy unchanged. The carried path is not free acceleration.','For a negative probe moving toward lower V, does potential energy rise or fall?');
add('24.2','An equipotential is a route with no voltage change, even if its length is nonzero.','Switch between the vertical along-plane path and the across-gap path; follow the labeled aqua voltage gates.','ΔV = −∫E·ds vanishes for motion perpendicular to E, but equals −EΔx across these uniform-field planes.','Changing plate spacing at fixed voltage changes E; it does not change the prescribed endpoint voltage fractions.','Why can a long sideways trip require zero work from the electric field?');
add('24.3','Potential is a scalar height; the field is a directed slope of that height.','Rotate the voltmeter arm at fixed radius, then shorten it. Only radial motion changes the voltage reading.','V = kq/r falls as 1/r, while E_r = kq/r² falls more rapidly. Both retain the source sign.','Infinity is the zero-voltage reference. The rotating measuring arm does not simulate an orbit.','At twice the radius, how do the voltage and field magnitudes compare with their starting values?');
add('24.4','Dipole potential compares two signed contributions with different distances.','Track the coral and aqua distance links as the probe crosses the equatorial plane. Equal distances give cancellation.','V_exact = kq(1/r₊ − 1/r₋); the distant approximation replaces this geometry with kp cosθ/r².','The far-field value requires r much larger than d; zero potential on the equator does not imply zero field.','Which source must be nearer when the exact potential is positive?');
add('24.5','Every source strip contributes its own charge divided by its own distance.','Follow the highlighted strip and its coral d+x bracket; compare uniform charge with charge concentrated toward the far end.','dq = λ(x)dx and dV = k dq/(d+x) explain why the total is an integral, not Q divided by an average distance.','The 16-strip midpoint sum is approximate; the exact integral reading remains separately labeled. The source scan is not charge flow.','With Q fixed, why should moving more charge toward the far end reduce its influence on the near probe?');
add('24.6','The field depends on how potential changes nearby, not on the potential value alone.','Compare the four sampling points around the held positive probe while changing a or b.','Differentiating V = ax²+by² gives E_x = −2ax and E_y = −2by; the coral resultant combines those signed slopes.','The coarse colored tiles illustrate sampled positions. The model field is analytic, and the probe is held stationary.','Can V be zero at a point where one or both field components are nonzero?');
add('24.7','Assembly energy is a sum over distinct pairs; adding the third charge introduces two new interactions.','Follow the bottom pair and the two sloping links, retaining each displayed energy sign.','W_add3 = q₃k(q₁+q₂)/a equals U₁₃+U₂₃, whereas the total also includes U₁₂.','Charges are brought quasistatically from infinity and then held fixed. Self energies are excluded.','If q₁+q₂ is zero, can adding q₃ cost zero work while total system energy remains nonzero?');
add('24.8','Zero field means a flat potential, not necessarily a zero potential.','Carry the meter through the metal: its reading stays at the surface value until the probe reaches the exterior.','E = −dV/dr is zero on the interior plateau. Outside, V = kQ/r joins continuously to kQ/R.','This isolated spherical conductor has no external field or cavity charge. Surface E uses the outward limit.','Why does moving inside the metal change neither voltage nor field even when Q is nonzero?');
add('25.1','Capacitance measures how much separated charge a geometry holds per volt.','Change plate area and separation; the real width and gap respond while equal opposite plate signs remain paired.','C = ε₀A/d sets geometry; Q = CV sets the amount of separated charge at the chosen voltage.','The ideal gap is insulating. The capacitor’s net charge is zero even when each plate has a substantial charge magnitude.','At fixed source voltage, which stores more charge: twice the area or twice the gap?');
add('25.2','A coaxial gap has different field strengths at different radii, so its voltage comes from an integral.','Compare the inner coral cylinder and aqua outer shell as their radius ratio and common length change.','E ∝ 1/r integrates to ln(b/a), giving C = 2πε₀L/ln(b/a).','This is the long, coaxial vacuum model with end effects neglected; b must stay larger than a.','Why does making the gap narrower increase capacitance without changing the conductor material?');
add('25.3','Wiring determines what two capacitors must share: voltage or charge magnitude.','Switch arrangements and trace the actual nodes before reading either equivalent capacitance or plate signs.','Parallel capacitors share V, so charges add. Series capacitors share charge magnitude, so voltage drops add.','The ideal capacitors and connecting wires introduce no leakage or parasitic capacitance.','Why must a series combination have a smaller equivalent capacitance than either member?');
add('25.4','Charging gets harder as accumulated plate charge raises the voltage.','Double voltage and compare field strength with stored-energy dots, whose reference stays fixed.','Integrating dU = (q/C)dq gives U = ½CV². The same energy equals uAd with u = ½ε₀E².','Energy belongs to the field-filled gap. Dot area is an energy encoding, not a count of physical particles.','Why does twice the voltage produce four times the stored energy rather than twice?');
add('25.5','Polarization raises capacitance, but the battery connection decides what changes next.','Insert the dielectric under both electrical constraints; compare plate signs, bound signs and field response.','The filled and unfilled side-by-side areas add capacitance. Connected means fixed V; isolated means fixed Q.','Partial insertion is modeled as parallel regions with negligible edge effects, not a layered series dielectric.','Why can the same insertion lower voltage in one experiment but increase plate charge in the other?');
add('25.6','Bound dielectric charge opposes the free-plate field without allowing free charge to cross the gap.','Increase κ and watch the opposing bound signs strengthen while the separate net-field arrow shortens.','D = κε₀E = σ_free keeps free charge distinct from the total-charge Gauss-law expression ε₀E = σ_free − |σ_bound|.','The gap is fully filled by a linear dielectric. At fixed σ_free and κ, increasing gap changes V = Ed but not E.','Why does a larger plate separation raise the voltage while leaving the field unchanged here?');
add('26.1','Current counts charge crossing a surface per second; a junction cannot create or consume that charge.','Adjust the branch split and compare the two outgoing current arrows with their shared incoming arrow.','I = dQ/dt and I_in = I_upper+I_lower connect the counting plane to charge conservation.','Arrows use conventional current. Marker direction depends on the chosen carrier convention; marker speed is schematic.','If the upper branch receives more current, what must happen to the lower branch at fixed incoming current?');
add('26.2','A constriction carries the same charge per second through less area, requiring faster drift.','Narrow one side while comparing its representative streamlines, current density and electron drift with the wide side.','J = I/(πr²) and v_d = J/(ne) explain both the radius-squared dependence and the carrier-density dependence.','Drift is an average carrier motion, much slower than electrical signal propagation. The single steady-current path has no charge accumulation.','At fixed I and n, what happens to drift speed if a wire radius is halved?');
add('26.3','Resistance combines an object’s geometry with its material’s resistivity.','Change length, radius, material and controlled temperature separately; identify which belongs to geometry and which to ρ.','R = ρL/(πr²) turns a longer path into more resistance and a wider cross-section into less.','The stated linear temperature law is a local material approximation. The tiny test current is not a self-heating simulation.','Why does doubling radius have a larger resistance effect than doubling length?');
add('26.4','A steady average drift emerges from repeated acceleration interrupted by collisions.','Increase E first, then increase the mean free time τ; distinguish a changed drive from a changed material response.','J = ne²τE/m_e is linear in E when n and τ stay fixed, so ρ = m_e/(ne²τ) remains constant.','The animated paths are illustrative collision/drift motion, not a microscopic many-body calculation.','Why does doubling E double J without halving the resistivity?');
add('26.5','The same word resistance connects heating, carrier supply and persistent current, but these are three different experiments.','Compare the heater, semiconductor and isolated ring as separate panels; do not trace one circuit through all three.','P = V²/R describes the driven heater; σ = neμ describes carrier supply; an isolated zero-resistance ring has I²R = 0.','The ring is never connected across a nonzero voltage source. Its sharp 4 K threshold is an ideal teaching assumption.','Why would applying I = V/R with R = 0 be the wrong way to interpret the isolated ring?');
add('27.1','A battery gives each coulomb energy; every resistance spends part of that energy, including the battery’s own resistance.','Trace the same current around the loop and compare emf with terminal voltage as internal resistance rises.','I = emf/(R₁+R₂+r), and terminal V = emf−Ir. The signed rises and drops sum to zero.','The source emf is fixed and the resistances are ideal lumped elements. Wire motion markers do not indicate electron energy packets.','Can a battery retain the same emf while delivering a smaller terminal voltage?');
add('27.2','All branches meet the same two node voltages, but their currents need not point the same way.','Lower the middle-source emf until its current arrow reverses and the stronger source drives charge into it.','Each branch current follows its own emf-minus-node-voltage divided by resistance; the junction equation couples them.','A negative solved current means reversal relative to the chosen reference direction, not a failed solution.','How can a battery absorb energy while another battery supplies it in the same network?');
add('27.3','A real instrument becomes part of the circuit it measures.','Follow the ammeter in series and the voltmeter’s separate parallel leads; lower R_V to reveal extra meter current.','The ammeter adds series resistance, while the voltmeter changes the parallel equivalent across the load.','Ideal readings are approached as R_A tends to zero and R_V becomes very large; finite instruments can load the circuit.','Why should a good voltmeter draw very little current?');
add('27.4','Capacitor voltage changes continuously, so the resistor initially sees the largest driving voltage during charging.','Compare growing plate charge with shrinking current, then switch to the separately prepared discharge experiment.','The time scale τ = RC appears in the exponential; I = (V_source−V_C)/R connects the current to the remaining voltage.','No leakage or current through the dielectric is included. Charging and discharge modes have different initial conditions.','Why does the charging current approach zero even though the battery remains connected?');
add('28.1','Magnetic force is sideways to velocity, so it can redirect motion without increasing kinetic energy.','Rotate the coral velocity relative to the aqua field; inspect the dot/cross force glyph and reverse charge sign.','q(v×B) supplies both the perpendicular direction and the sinθ factor; F·v = 0.','The dot/cross describes the out-of-plane force component. Parallel motion has zero magnetic force even for nonzero B.','Why is the force largest at 90° but zero at 0° and 180°?');
add('28.2','A velocity selector balances two forces only at a particular entry speed.','Set v to E/B and compare the straight beam for positive and negative particles; then move slightly above or below it.','The initial transverse force is q(E−vB), so reversing q reverses both terms without changing the selected speed.','Away from balance, the model follows planar uniform-field motion; the magnetic force alone does no work.','Why can opposite charge signs share the same undeflected selected speed?');
add('28.3','Hall charge separation builds until the electric force balances the sideways magnetic force on the carriers.','Reverse the carrier type and watch which edge becomes positive while conventional current remains the stated reference.','I = nqv_xwt relates signed drift to current; V_H = −IB/(nqt) links the measured polarity to carrier sign.','The edge-polarity convention is top minus bottom. This steady-state balance omits the transient buildup.','At fixed current, why does a thinner strip produce a larger Hall voltage?');
add('28.4','The field continuously turns velocity toward the center without changing its speed.','Watch the tangent velocity and inward force as the ion moves; reverse charge to reverse circulation only.','|q|vB = mv²/r gives r = mv/(|q|B); the period 2πm/(|q|B) no longer contains v.','Motion is perpendicular to a uniform field and nonrelativistic. Orbit direction and radius encode different facts.','Why does a faster ion trace a larger circle without needing a longer period?');
add('28.5','The gap supplies energy in discrete crossings; the dees bend the ion between crossings.','Follow one constant-radius arc, then the next larger one after a gap crossing.','Each crossing adds eΔV, and r = √(2mK)/(eB); the nonrelativistic cyclotron frequency depends on B and m.','The widening path is not continuous acceleration inside a dee. Relativistic frequency changes and engineering losses are excluded.','Why can increasing gap voltage enlarge the orbit without changing the required oscillator frequency?');
add('28.6','A wire force is the collective magnetic force on moving charge, expressed with conventional current.','Reverse I or rotate the actual wire; inspect the out-of-plane force direction.','F = I(L×B) gives magnitude |I|LB|sinθ|, retaining the sign of the current reference.','The modeled segment is straight and the external field uniform; no flexible-wire bending dynamics are solved.','Could a live current-carrying wire feel zero magnetic force in a nonzero field?');
add('28.7','A current loop can have zero resultant force but a nonzero force couple.','Compare the equal opposite side-force arrows with their different lines of action as the loop orientation changes.','The side forces create τ = μ×B with μ = NIA n̂; the loop area enters through both force and lever arm.','The angle scan is imposed and does not predict motor angular speed. The external field is uniform.','Why can equal opposite forces rotate a loop instead of translating it?');
add('28.8','Torque tells you how energy changes with angle; it does not alone identify stable equilibrium.','Compare aligned, perpendicular and anti-aligned moments, looking at both U and τ.','U = −μB cosθ has slope −τ_z, so energy minima and maxima can both have zero torque.','The moment orientation is controlled. No angular inertia, damping or thermal motion is inferred from the scan.','Which zero-torque orientation returns after a small disturbance, and why?');
add('29.1','A straight current produces a circulating field, not a radial field toward or away from the wire.','Carry the probe around the wire and follow the tangent aqua arrow; then increase its radius.','B_φ = μ₀I/(2πr) combines the 1/r magnitude with right-hand circulation.','The infinite-wire approximation neglects end effects. The moving marker is a measurement scan rather than a charged-particle orbit.','Why does reversing current reverse every tangent arrow but leave its magnitude unchanged?');
add('29.2','Each wire feels the field made by the other; the sign product decides attraction or repulsion.','Reverse only one current and compare both mutual force arrows with the separate source-field arrow.','F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd) combines the source-wire field with the force on the receiving current.','These are long parallel straight wires. The drawn aqua field at one wire is not an additional mechanical force.','Why does reversing both currents preserve attraction or repulsion?');
add('29.3','Inside a thick wire, growing enclosed current competes with the growing circumference of the observation loop.','Move the red loop across the conductor surface and compare the highlighted enclosed area with tangent B.','I_enclosed ∝ r² inside, while the circulation path is 2πr; therefore B ∝ r inside and 1/r outside.','Current density is uniform. The two field formulas meet continuously at r = R.','Why does moving outward first strengthen the field and then weaken it?');
add('29.4','The same winding current gives different fields when its path is a long cylinder or a closed ring.','Switch solenoid to toroid and trace the physical winding and the relevant Ampèrian path.','A long solenoid gives B = μ₀NI/ℓ; a toroid gives B = μ₀NI/(2πr) within its winding region.','Changing bore radius need not change ideal long-solenoid B at fixed N, ℓ and I. Edge and leakage fields are neglected.','Why does radial position matter for the ideal toroid but not for the ideal solenoid interior?');
add('29.5','A current loop looks like a magnetic dipole only from sufficiently far away.','Move the axial probe away from the real coil and compare exact and far-field readings without replacing one by the other.','The exact field contains (R²+z²)^(−3/2); only for z much larger than R does it reduce to the dipole 1/z³ law.','At z = 0 the exact field is finite. The far-field formula is not valid there.','Why should increasing distance improve the dipole approximation even while weakening the field?');
add('30.1','Induction responds to changing linked flux, not simply to the presence of a magnet.','Reverse the magnet’s approach speed and compare the signed emf with the coil’s inset current orientation.','ε = −N dΦ/dt combines how flux depends on separation with how separation changes in time.','The dipole-source approximation and fixed 10 Ω loop apply. Current arrows are schematic; no free particle trajectory is implied.','If the magnet pauses at a strong-field position, should an emf persist?');
add('30.2','Your mechanical work can become resistor heat through the moving conductor and its induced current.','Compare the rightward pull with leftward magnetic drag; set speed to zero to remove emf and heating.','ε = Bℓv drives I = ε/R; the drag force IℓB makes P_pull = Fv equal I²R.','Speed is prescribed by an external pull. The model does not solve an unforced decelerating rod.','Why does doubling speed quadruple power rather than merely doubling it?');
add('30.3','Changing magnetic flux produces a circulating electric field, including outside the region containing B.','Move the coral observation ring across the aqua disk boundary and follow the signed tangent arrows.','2πrE_φ = −π min(r²,R²)dB/dt gives E ∝ r inside and E ∝ 1/r outside.','This electric field is nonconservative; it cannot be described globally by a single electrostatic potential difference around the loop.','How can the field be nonzero at a point where the local magnetic field is zero?');
add('30.4','Inductance counts flux linkage per current, so geometry can set L even when no current flows.','Change turns and bore separately, then set I to zero: the winding remains a definite geometry.','B = μ₀NI/ℓ and NΦ = NBA combine to L = μ₀N²A/ℓ.','This is an ideal long vacuum solenoid. The steady-current scan does not create a self-induced emf.','Why does doubling turns produce four times the inductance?');
add('30.5','Self-induction opposes a change of current, which is different from always opposing the current itself.','Choose a nonzero I₀ with zero slope, then reverse only the ramp slope and compare the emf arrow.','ε_self = −L dI/dt is determined by slope; U = ½LI² depends on current magnitude.','The controlled source prescribes a linear current ramp. A nonzero current can coexist with zero induced emf.','During decreasing positive current, why can the induced emf support the existing current?');
add('30.6','An inductor keeps current continuous while its voltage polarity can reverse between growth and decay.','Compare the battery branch with the decay bypass; follow current direction and coil terminal polarity separately.','τ = L/R sets the exponential response, while V_source−RI−L dI/dt = 0 fixes each voltage sign.','Decay is a separately prepared initial experiment, not a live switch preserving the immediately preceding current value.','Why does the current continue in the same direction after the inductor voltage reverses?');
add('30.7','The resistor receives energy that was stored in the coil’s magnetic field.','Watch coil-storage glyphs shrink while thermal glyphs accumulate at the resistor; compare their sum.','I = I₀e^(−Rt/L) gives dU/dt = −I²R, so U_field + Q_heat remains the initial ½LI₀².','The source is disconnected and resistance is fixed. Energy-glyph area represents joules, not moving material.','How can total accounted energy remain constant while current and stored field energy decrease?');
add('30.8','Field strength sets energy density; the amount of field-filled space sets how much total energy there is.','Change cylinder size at fixed B, then change B at fixed size. Compare density and total-energy readings.','u_B = B²/(2μ₀) and U = u_Bπr²ℓ separate a local property from an extensive total.','The field is uniform in vacuum and independently prescribed. Fringe energy and magnetic materials are excluded.','Why can doubling length double total energy without changing local density?');
add('30.9','A secondary winding can develop voltage without carrying current when its circuit is open.','Keep the secondary terminal gap visible while reversing the primary current ramp.','N₂Φ₂₁ = MI₁ differentiates to ε₂ = −M dI₁/dt; M grows with both turn counts and shared area.','Ideal common flux and winding orientation set the sign. The slight drawing offset is not a leakage-field model.','Why does a nonzero induced voltage not justify drawing moving carriers in the open secondary?');
add('31.1','Energy alternates between the capacitor’s electric field and the coil’s magnetic field.','Start with full plate charge and zero current; at empty plates inspect the largest current and magnetic storage.','q = CV₀ cosω₀t and i = dq/dt are a quarter-cycle apart, while q²/(2C)+Li²/2 remains constant.','The loop is isolated and resistance-free. Playback represents model time; energy-glyph area shares one fixed reference.','Why can current be largest exactly when capacitor charge passes through zero?');
add('31.2','Resistance opens an irreversible energy path to heat while the remaining fields continue exchanging energy.','Compare successive field-storage peaks and the accumulating heat; check zero initial current.','Lq″+Rq′+q/C = 0 gives d(U_E+U_B)/dt = −Ri². The initial-condition phase matters.','Controls stay underdamped. A simple decaying cosine alone would not satisfy both the specified initial charge and zero initial current.','Why can electromagnetic energy decrease monotonically even though charge repeatedly reverses sign?');
add('31.3','A derivative changes timing: a resistor follows voltage, a capacitor responds to its slope, and an inductor integrates it.','Use the common positive voltage peak at t = 0; compare which current is zero and which way it moves next.','i_R = v/R, i_C = C dv/dt and v_L = L di/dt explain the lead/lag without memorizing arrows.','Only the selected ideal component is connected. The same source cosine is used in every comparison.','Why does increasing frequency change capacitive and inductive current amplitudes in opposite ways?');
add('31.4','Series components share one current, but their voltage drops need not peak together.','Sweep frequency across the balance point and compare the opposing inductive and capacitive effects.','X_L = ωL and X_C = 1/(ωC) combine as their difference in Z = √[R²+(X_L−X_C)²].','Sinusoidal steady state is assumed. Reactive voltage amplitudes cannot simply be added as positive scalar magnitudes.','At resonance, how can coil and capacitor voltages be large while their combined reactive drop is zero?');
add('31.5','A reactive element returns some energy each cycle; the resistor only converts energy into heat.','Follow the source-power arrow as it reverses while checking that resistor heating stays nonnegative.','P_avg = V_rms I_rms cosφ = I_rms²R separates average real power from apparent volt-amperes.','The stated net reactance is an ideal capacitor or coil equivalent at the operating frequency. RMS values describe cycle averages, not instantaneous power.','Why can V_rms times I_rms overestimate the average power converted to heat?');
add('31.6','A transformer trades voltage for current through shared magnetic flux; it does not create power.','Change the turns ratio, then adjust load while comparing the separate winding currents and generator voltage drop.','V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p and ideal power balance imply the inverse current ratio; the reflected load scales with the square of turns ratio.','The windings are electrically isolated and ideally coupled. The generator’s series resistance is included separately.','Why might adding secondary turns fail to increase delivered load power from a finite-resistance source?');
add('32.1','A closed boundary cannot trap net magnetic flux from an ordinary magnetic field.','Compare the two outward-normal arrows and their equal opposite end-face fluxes as area changes.','The signed terms +BA and −BA add to ∮B·dA = 0. A larger area changes both equally.','The field is uniform, and the entire closed surface is included; zero net flux does not mean zero B.','Why would counting only the outgoing end face give the wrong conclusion?');
add('32.2','A changing electric field contributes to magnetic circulation even without material current through the gap.','Move the measurement loop across the plate edge, then reverse dE/dt and watch the tangent B direction reverse.','Ampère–Maxwell uses μ₀ε₀ dΦ_E/dt, with enclosed area π min(r²,R²).','The field-filled disk and symmetry are idealized. B is continuous at r = R although its radial trend changes.','Why does increasing loop radius outside the plate make B smaller even though enclosed changing flux is unchanged?');
add('32.3','Conduction current in the wire and changing electric flux in the gap give a consistent magnetic-field account.','Follow carriers up to the plate; none cross the vacuum. Compare the loop’s enclosed fraction with total displacement current.','I = ε₀A dE/dt equals the full-gap displacement-current term; smaller loops enclose only part of that flux.','Displacement current is a field term, not electrons jumping between plates. The ideal uniform-field approximation neglects fringing.','How can two different spanning surfaces lead to the same magnetic circulation?');
add('32.4','Declination turns the horizontal direction; inclination tilts the same field below or above the horizon.','Compare the compass and dip needle while changing D and I independently.','B_horizontal = B cosI and B_up = −B sinI preserve B² = B_horizontal²+B_up².','The model describes a chosen local vector, not a prediction of Earth’s field at a geographic location.','Why can a horizontal compass fail to show the full field magnitude?');
add('32.5','For an electron, magnetic moment and spin angular momentum point oppositely.','Compare the two projection arrows and the selected energy level; increase B to widen the splitting.','μ_z = −2m_sμ_B and U = −μ_zB give two allowed energies with separation 2μ_BB.','These are quantum projections, not a spinning classical ball tracing a cone. The stated g ≈ 2 approximation is used.','Which spin projection has its magnetic moment aligned with a positive z field?');
add('32.6','A diamagnet develops an opposing moment, and a gradient converts that response into a net force.','Keep B nonzero while setting its gradient to zero; the induced moment remains but the force disappears.','μ_induced = χVB/μ₀ and F_y ≈ (χV/μ₀)B(dB/dy) retain the negative susceptibility sign.','This is a weak, linear diamagnetic sample in a slowly varying applied field. Its motion is not integrated.','Why does an opposing moment alone not guarantee a translational force in a uniform field?');
add('32.7','Heat competes with the field’s preference for microscopic alignment.','Increase temperature at fixed B and compare the schematic lattice with the fixed-scale net-magnetization arrow.','Curie’s law M = CB/T predicts the net response; keeping B fixed makes the inverse-temperature trend explicit.','This is the weak-field Curie approximation. Individual arrows illustrate an ensemble rather than exact thermal trajectories.','If both B and T double, what happens to the predicted magnetization?');
add('32.8','A core’s measured magnetization can contribute a field even when winding current is zero.','Set I to zero while keeping alignment nonzero, then reverse the alignment independently of the winding current.','B = μ₀(nI+M) adds the winding contribution and the material-state contribution with their signs.','Alignment is an independently specified measured state. This model does not infer a hysteresis curve from current alone.','Why is zero current insufficient to conclude that the core’s total field is zero?');
add('33.1','An electromagnetic wave consists of linked perpendicular fields whose changing values travel through space.','Track the electric arrow and magnetic dot/cross at the same sample point; both reverse phase together.','E_m/B_m = c links amplitudes, and E×B fixes propagation direction; fλ = c links timing to wavelength.','Playback is deliberately slowed. The field samples are not material particles moving with the wave.','If the wavelength doubles in vacuum, which changes: wave speed, frequency, or both?');
add('33.2','Intensity is energy per area per time; detector power also depends on how much area catches the beam.','Change E at fixed detector area, then enlarge the detector at fixed E.','I = E_m²/(2μ₀c) makes intensity quadratic in field amplitude, while P_avg = IA is linear in collecting area.','The beam is an ideal uniform plane wave over the illuminated detector area. Peak field and cycle-average intensity are distinct.','Why does doubling detector area leave intensity unchanged but double collected power?');
add('33.3','Reflection transfers more momentum because the light reverses its direction instead of simply stopping.','Switch the sail from absorbing to reflecting and compare the return beam with the force reading.','Momentum flux gives p = I/c for absorption and p = 2I/c for ideal normal reflection; F = pA.','Incidence is normal and response ideal. Sail acceleration and thermal re-emission are not modeled.','Why can two sails feel different force under the same incident intensity and area?');
add('33.4','The analyzer passes one field component; brightness follows the square of that component.','Rotate the aqua transmission axis through 90° and compare the surviving outgoing field with intensity.','E_out = E_in cosθ becomes I_out = I_in cos²θ. A negative field projection still gives nonnegative intensity.','The input is already linearly polarized. The extra factor of one half applies only when starting with unpolarized light.','Why do 60° and 120° transmit the same intensity even though their field projections have opposite signs?');
add('33.5','Refraction changes a ray’s direction because phase must match along the shared boundary.','Measure both angles from the faint normal, not the surface, while changing the two refractive indices.','n₁ sinθ₁ = n₂ sinθ₂ predicts bending toward the normal in the higher-index medium.','This is ray optics for ideal transparent media. If the transmitted sine would exceed one, there is no propagating refracted ray.','Why is there no bend when n₁ equals n₂?');
add('33.6','Total internal reflection is a boundary beyond which no transmitted propagating direction satisfies Snell’s law.','Increase incidence from the higher-index side; the refracted ray first approaches the surface, then vanishes.','The limiting condition sinθ_c = n₂/n₁ exists only when n₁ > n₂.','The absence of a propagating transmitted ray does not mean every electromagnetic field at the boundary vanishes; evanescent detail is outside this ray model.','Can light entering a higher-index medium have a total-internal-reflection critical angle?');
add('33.7','At a special incidence, the reflected light loses its p-polarized component.','Compare the coral reflected and aqua transmitted rays as the indices set Brewster’s angle.','tanθ_B = n₂/n₁ makes the outgoing ray directions perpendicular: θ_B+θ_t = 90°.','The ideal dielectric boundary has no absorption. The dot/cross represents transverse electric polarization, not moving charges.','If the second medium’s index rises, should Brewster’s angle rise or fall?');
add('34.1','A virtual image is where reflected rays appear to come from when extended backward.','Trace the real coral paths first, then their faint extensions to the aqua image behind the mirror.','Reflection symmetry gives i = −p and m = −i/p = +1, preserving upright height.','Light does not actually converge behind the mirror. The negative image distance labels the virtual location.','Why can you see an image at a point that no reflected ray physically reaches?');
add('34.2','The sign convention and object position decide whether reflected rays truly meet or only their extensions do.','Move the object across the concave focal distance, then switch mirror type and compare image orientation.','1/p+1/i = 1/f gives i = fp/(p−f); m = −i/p converts distance sign into orientation.','The paraxial mirror model omits spherical aberration. At p = f the ideal finite image position is replaced by an image at infinity.','Why does an object inside a concave mirror’s focal length have an upright virtual image?');
add('34.3','One curved refracting surface is not the same optical system as a complete thin lens.','Follow rays through the single air-to-glass boundary while changing curvature and refractive index.','n₁/p+n₂/i = (n₂−n₁)/R balances object divergence with the surface’s refracting power.','Distances use the stated paraxial relation. The displayed ray height is illustrative and may be exaggerated.','What happens to the surface’s optical power as its radius becomes very large?');
add('34.4','A thin lens bends rays so the same signed equation can describe real and virtual images.','Trace the construction rays before inspecting the image arrow; change object distance through the focal region.','1/p+1/i = 1/f determines location, then m = −i/p determines relative height and orientation.','The thin, paraxial model excludes aberrations and finite lens thickness. Near p = f, image distance becomes very large.','Why does a diverging lens form a virtual upright image for an ordinary real object?');
add('34.5','A telescope changes viewing angle by sharing an intermediate focal plane between two lenses.','Change either focal length and compare both the lens separation and outgoing angular spread.','The objective forms h′ ≈ f_objective θ_object; the eyepiece turns that height into angle, giving m_θ = −f_objective/f_eyepiece.','This is an astronomical telescope adjusted for relaxed viewing with a distant source; the negative sign indicates inversion.','Why can a longer objective increase magnification while also lengthening the instrument?');
add('34.6','The small-angle geometry makes all ray-height factors cancel, leaving a common paraxial image.','Change only h and follow the faint normal toward C; the ray angles change but the predicted image location does not.','α+γ = 2β with α ≈ h/p, β ≈ h/R and γ ≈ h/i gives 1/p+1/i = 2/R.','The cancellation is paraxial. Large-angle spherical aberration is not predicted by the small-angle equation.','Why is changing the ray height not supposed to change the image distance in this model?');
add('35.1','A transparent slab delays phase by adding optical path, even when the geometric paths are equal.','Compare wavefront spacing inside the slab with the reference arm in air and the outgoing relative phase.','The extra path is (n−1)t, so Δφ = 2π(n−1)t/λ₀; frequency stays fixed while in-medium wavelength becomes λ₀/n.','The paths are coherent and the medium is nondispersive at the chosen wavelength. Playback is an illustrative phase scan.','Why is nt alone not the extra optical path relative to the same thickness of air?');
add('35.2','Bright bands mark screen positions where the two coherent paths differ by whole wavelengths.','Change slit separation, wavelength and screen distance one at a time while using the fixed millimeter screen scale.','d sinθ = mλ gives the bright directions; for small angles, neighboring bands are separated by approximately λL/d.','The slits are coherent and equally illuminated. The simple fringe-spacing relation is a small-angle approximation.','Why do wider-spaced slits make the screen fringes closer together?');
add('35.3','Two fields combine before brightness is calculated, so phase can turn reinforcement into cancellation.','Follow the phasor addition and the screen together; vary extra phase separately from slit spacing.','φ_total = 2πd sinθ/λ+φ_extra feeds I/I_max = cos²(φ_total/2). At the center only the extra phase remains.','This is equal-amplitude coherent two-source interference; the phasor is a field representation rather than a particle orbit.','Why does changing slit spacing alter the off-axis pattern while leaving the central extra-phase result unchanged?');
add('35.4','Thin-film interference counts both travel through the film and the phase change on reflection.','Follow the front reflection’s phase reversal and the rear ray’s round trip as thickness changes.','Propagation adds 4πnt/λ₀ and the air–film–air arrangement adds one π reflection difference. Both enter the final phase.','The model uses normal incidence and a simplified two-reflection equal-amplitude comparison; it does not sum every internal reflection.','Why is zero thickness dark at the chosen reflected port rather than automatically bright?');
add('35.5','Moving one mirror changes the outward trip and the return trip, doubling the path change.','Track the right mirror’s real displacement and compare it with the recombined detector’s phase cycles.','ΔOPL = 2Δx gives N = 2Δx/λ complete fringe cycles; a whole cycle restores the chosen port’s starting brightness.','The arms are coherent and ideal. A signed phase shift and a nonnegative count of passed fringes are different quantities.','How far must one mirror move to produce one full bright-to-bright cycle?');
add('36.1','A narrow slit has more diffraction spread because its first complete cancellation occurs farther from the axis.','Narrow the physical aperture and compare both dark edges of the central screen region on its fixed millimeter scale.','Pairing slit halves gives a sinθ₁ = λ; the central width is twice L tanθ₁.','Uniform coherent illumination and a distant screen are assumed. Diffraction is a wave effect, not particles bouncing off slit edges.','Why does making the opening smaller make the central light region wider?');
add('36.2','Different parts of one slit reach an off-axis point with different phases, so their field contributions may cancel.','Follow the head-to-tail wavelet construction at the marked screen position as viewing angle changes.','β = πa sinθ/λ sets the total phase spread; the field factor sinβ/β is squared to obtain intensity.','At β = 0 use the continuous limit one, not division by zero. The phasors represent field contributions.','Why must fields be added before squaring to predict a dark minimum?');
add('36.3','Even a perfect finite lens spreads each star into an extended diffraction spot.','Change aperture diameter and compare spot size with the fixed angular separation of the two stars.','The first Airy minimum is at approximately 1.22λ/D; Rayleigh’s criterion compares that width with the star separation.','The criterion is a useful resolution convention, not an abrupt physical switch between one source and two.','Why can a larger objective distinguish two stars without changing their actual angular separation?');
add('36.4','Finite slit width shapes a broad diffraction envelope while slit separation creates finer interference fringes inside it.','Change width and separation independently; identify which moves the broad envelope and which crowds the fine bands.','The product (sinβ/β)² cos²δ keeps the single-slit envelope distinct from two-slit interference.','A nominal interference maximum can disappear at a diffraction zero. Equal coherent slit illumination and the far-field model are assumed.','How can a predicted double-slit bright order be missing from the real pattern?');
add('36.5','Many coherent openings reinforce sharply in selected directions rather than creating arbitrary extra brightness bands.','Increase illuminated slit count at fixed spacing; line centers stay put while their angular width narrows.','d sinθ = mλ fixes principal directions, while the first-minimum width scales approximately as 1/N.','The displayed orders are kept propagating by the control range. Representative drawing marks need not equal every physical slit.','Why do more illuminated slits improve sharpness without changing the principal order angles?');
add('36.6','Spectral resolution requires line separation to compete successfully with diffraction width.','Change N while holding wavelength separation fixed; compare narrower line extents with unchanged center separation.','Angular dispersion moves the centers, whereas λ/(Nd cosθ) sets local width; resolving power combines them as mN.','The local angular display is deliberately magnified. Rayleigh resolution is a convention rather than a sudden loss of light.','Why can adding rulings resolve two lines even if their center angles barely change?');
add('36.7','Adjacent crystal planes add an extra down-and-back path whose phase decides reinforcement.','Measure the drawn glancing angle from the planes, then increase plane spacing at fixed wavelength.','The two-ray path difference is 2d sinθ; first-order reinforcement requires it to equal λ.','This is ideal first-order Bragg interference. The glancing angle is measured from the planes, unlike the normal-based refraction angle.','Why does larger plane spacing require a smaller first-order glancing angle?');
window.GlassPhysicsVisualGuides=Object.assign(window.GlassPhysicsVisualGuides||{},G);
})();
